            <ArticleTitle>Pudendal nerve conduction to evaluate organic erectile dysfunction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>885-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study aimed to evaluate the value of testing pudendal nerve conduction in men with erectile dysfunction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">This open prospective study was conducted on 150 men with documented erectile dysfunction of at least 6 mo of duration, all of whom underwent pudendal nerve conduction by the same investigator.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Only patients with erectile dysfunction and low back pain (n = 9) showed statistically significant prolonged pathologic pudendal nerve conduction latency of 43.1 msec/div (SD +/- 11.3 msec/div, P &lt; 0.05). There were no correlations between the pudendal nerve conduction latency in patients suffering from erectile dysfunction associated with diabetes mellitus, local trauma, pelvic surgery, or antihypertensive drugs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Pudendal nerve conduction may contribute valuable information to the evaluation of patients with erectile dysfunction caused by neurologic deficits as manifested by low back pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
